WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.987
How long would it take you to go all the way round the world?

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:13.491
Today, about forty-four hours.

00:00:14.508 --> 00:00:20.319
In November twenty twenty-four, three men from Hong Kong went the whole way round on

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:24.193
ordinary scheduled flights, on tickets anyone could buy.

00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:29.263
Higher up it is faster.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.526
The space station laps the planet every ninety minutes.

00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:38.315
The people on board watch sixteen sunrises a day.

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:41.497
Now take all of that away.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:43.583
No aircraft.

00:00:44.264 --> 00:00:45.395
No radio.

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:50.036
In eighteen seventy-two the honest answer was: nobody knew.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.593
So somebody turned it into a bet.

00:00:54.519 --> 00:00:58.894
Eighty days, half a fortune — and for the next hundred and fifty years,

00:00:58.894 --> 00:01:01.111
real people kept trying to beat him.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.731
Which is strange — because he never existed.

00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:18.187
The book came out, and people began treating a novel as a challenge.

00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:24.710
An American called George Francis Train insisted he had already done it,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:29.010
two years before the book, and spent the rest of his life saying so.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:38.630
Then in eighteen eighty-nine a New York reporter named Nellie Bly was given two

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.194
days' notice, one bag and a ticket, and sent east.

00:01:46.327 --> 00:01:50.124
On the way she stopped at Amiens and met Jules Verne himself.

00:01:51.995 --> 00:01:56.520
She came home in seventy-two days, six hours and eleven minutes

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.035
— and beat a man who did not exist.

00:02:02.264 --> 00:02:07.392
What she did not know is that a rival magazine had put a second woman on a train the

00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:09.773
same day, going the opposite way round.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:16.058
Elizabeth Bisland lost by four days.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:19.093
She also beat Fogg.

00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:21.221
It never stopped.

00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:27.424
In nineteen twenty-eight a Danish paper ran a competition in Verne's honour and sent

00:02:27.424 --> 00:02:31.878
the winner — a boy of fifteen — round the world with aircraft banned.

00:02:36.297 --> 00:02:37.641
Forty-four days.

00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:41.940
Twenty thousand people met him at Copenhagen station.

00:02:43.668 --> 00:02:46.463
And there is a sailing trophy named after Verne.

00:02:47.914 --> 00:02:52.252
The first time anyone won it, the time was seventy-nine days:

00:02:52.252 --> 00:02:54.527
under eighty by seventeen hours.

00:02:57.578 --> 00:02:59.285
It now stands at forty.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.515
So who were they racing?

00:03:11.396 --> 00:03:12.356
A book.

00:03:13.017 --> 00:03:16.089
Around the World in Eighty Days, by Jules Verne.

00:03:17.588 --> 00:03:21.210
It ran as a serial in the Paris newspaper Le Temps,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:27.034
starting on the sixth of November, eighteen seventy-two — while, inside the story,

00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:29.023
Fogg was out there doing it.

00:03:33.503 --> 00:03:36.959
And Verne timed the ending to land on the real calendar.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.682
The instalments ran up to the twentieth of December.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:46.722
On the twenty-first — Fogg's deadline — there was nothing at all.

00:03:48.407 --> 00:03:51.607
The last two chapters appeared on the twenty-second.

00:03:53.156 --> 00:03:55.823
Some readers thought it was actually happening.

00:03:57.161 --> 00:03:58.356
Bets were placed.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:02.694
Shipping companies wrote to Verne asking to be put in it.

00:04:04.396 --> 00:04:06.788
The book came out complete six weeks later,

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.068
and it has never been out of print since.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.480
Its hero spends the whole journey trying not to look at the world he is crossing.

00:04:25.987 --> 00:04:29.507
London, the second of October, eighteen seventy-two.

00:04:31.538 --> 00:04:36.752
In a club on Pall Mall, Phileas Fogg bets five other members half of everything he

00:04:36.752 --> 00:04:41.586
owns that he can go round the Earth and be back in that room in eighty days.

00:04:46.066 --> 00:04:47.901
Here is the part that matters.

00:04:49.093 --> 00:04:50.949
He is not betting on himself.

00:04:52.152 --> 00:04:56.894
He is betting on the planet — on whether the railways and steamers and canals

00:04:56.894 --> 00:05:01.944
finished in the last three years have actually joined up into one working machine.

00:05:06.424 --> 00:05:08.877
His friends think the world is still too big.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.125
He thinks it has just closed.

00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:17.478
And in eighteen seventy-two that was a genuinely open question.

00:05:19.848 --> 00:05:22.344
Which is why it is a bet and not a boast.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.987
Three years earlier the bet would have been impossible.

00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:49.242
Not hard.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.001
Impossible.

00:05:51.655 --> 00:05:55.406
May eighteen sixty-nine: two railways meet in Utah,

00:05:55.406 --> 00:05:58.567
and America has a line from coast to coast.

00:06:01.097 --> 00:06:06.046
November: the Suez Canal opens, and ships stop going round Africa.

00:06:07.937 --> 00:06:12.673
Eighteen seventy: India's railways join up across the subcontinent.

00:06:14.494 --> 00:06:18.889
September eighteen seventy-one: a tunnel opens under the Alps.

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:24.226
And on the twenty-second of August, eighteen seventy-two

00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:27.530
— six weeks before Fogg walks out of his front door

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.675
— the last stretch of telegraph wire is strung across Australia,

00:06:31.675 --> 00:06:36.791
and for the first time a message can go the whole way round the world and back.

00:06:41.271 --> 00:06:44.556
The last inhabited continent joins the network.

00:06:45.906 --> 00:06:47.314
The circuit closes.

00:06:48.052 --> 00:06:49.481
And then he sets off.

00:06:50.226 --> 00:06:52.594
That circuit is the route of this series.

00:06:53.645 --> 00:06:58.262
Eight episodes, one lap: the Channel and the Alps, the canal at Suez,

00:06:58.262 --> 00:07:03.750
the railway across India with a piece missing, the ports of the East, the Pacific,

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.357
the line across America.

00:07:09.448 --> 00:07:13.501
And all the way round, the question Fogg is actually betting on.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.828
Had the world really closed?

00:07:17.671 --> 00:07:21.639
We are going to find out where it held — and where it very nearly didn't.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.925
Fogg is setting out from the biggest city that had ever existed.

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:42.717
London, eighteen seventy-one: three point nine million people.

00:07:44.431 --> 00:07:46.223
A quarter bigger than Beijing.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.912
Two-thirds bigger than Paris.

00:07:49.809 --> 00:07:51.366
Five times New York.

00:07:52.183 --> 00:07:53.847
The world's largest port.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:58.839
Twelve main railway termini — no other city had anything close.

00:08:00.545 --> 00:08:03.545
And an underground railway, the first anywhere,

00:08:03.545 --> 00:08:08.268
nine years old and already carrying nine and a half million people a year.

00:08:11.209 --> 00:08:14.046
Here is what people get wrong about Fogg's London.

00:08:15.304 --> 00:08:16.520
It was not old.

00:08:17.219 --> 00:08:19.118
The Embankment was two years old.

00:08:20.052 --> 00:08:21.631
His station was eight.

00:08:22.455 --> 00:08:24.268
The clock tower was thirteen.

00:08:25.173 --> 00:08:29.673
He set out from a brand-new city to find out whether the rest of the world had

00:08:29.673 --> 00:08:30.250
caught up.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.032
Everything left in this episode happens inside half a mile.

00:08:45.227 --> 00:08:49.344
Verne gives Fogg three addresses, all in the same corner of London.

00:08:51.579 --> 00:08:53.328
A house on Savile Row.

00:08:54.439 --> 00:08:55.975
A club on Pall Mall.

00:08:56.984 --> 00:08:58.861
A station at Charing Cross.

00:09:00.032 --> 00:09:04.789
He walks the first leg himself, and because he is the man he is, he counts it.

00:09:07.329 --> 00:09:09.740
Eleven hundred and fifty-one steps.

00:09:11.165 --> 00:09:13.000
About nine hundred metres.

00:09:14.151 --> 00:09:16.391
That is the whole of London he uses.

00:09:17.735 --> 00:09:22.578
A man about to go round the Earth spends his last day on it inside half a mile.

00:09:25.158 --> 00:09:27.467
So we are going to walk that half mile

00:09:27.467 --> 00:09:32.571
— because all three of those places turn out to be more interesting than the man who

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:33.179
used them.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.792
Fogg makes the bet in a club.

00:09:47.917 --> 00:09:50.178
It is worth knowing what that word meant.

00:09:51.525 --> 00:09:52.570
Not a bar.

00:09:53.343 --> 00:09:58.818
A private house the size of a palace, where a few hundred men who agreed with each

00:09:58.818 --> 00:10:04.359
other could eat, read, write letters and in many of them sleep — all of it staffed,

00:10:04.359 --> 00:10:05.695
none of it for sale.

00:10:10.175 --> 00:10:11.946
You could not buy your way in.

00:10:13.061 --> 00:10:15.920
You were proposed, seconded, then voted on.

00:10:17.549 --> 00:10:22.648
Every member dropped a ball into a box: white to admit, black to reject.

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:27.402
Enough black balls and you were out.

00:10:28.658 --> 00:10:30.621
That is where blackballed comes from.

00:10:31.827 --> 00:10:36.097
At its height London had more than four hundred of these places,

00:10:36.097 --> 00:10:40.168
most inside a few hundred metres of St James's and Pall Mall.

00:10:44.382 --> 00:10:49.266
This is the second leg of Fogg's walk, and almost every building on it is still

00:10:49.266 --> 00:10:49.822
standing.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.693
The room where Fogg makes his bet is, in real life, a political weapon.

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:12.755
After the Reform Act of eighteen thirty-two,

00:11:12.755 --> 00:11:17.641
the Conservatives founded the Carlton Club, a few doors along Pall Mall.

00:11:20.933 --> 00:11:25.485
The other side wanted something to counter the machinations of the Carlton

00:11:25.485 --> 00:11:28.869
— their words — and in eighteen thirty-six they got it.

00:11:32.185 --> 00:11:37.197
So the room where a fictional Englishman bets half his fortune on the size of the

00:11:37.197 --> 00:11:40.228
world is the headquarters of a political faction,

00:11:40.228 --> 00:11:43.321
built to out-build the opposition down the street.

00:11:47.801 --> 00:11:49.806
It is still there, still a club.

00:11:50.854 --> 00:11:56.304
In nineteen eighty-one it became one of the first of the old clubs to admit women on

00:11:56.304 --> 00:11:57.083
equal terms.

00:11:59.746 --> 00:12:03.199
Its library, given book by book by its own members,

00:12:03.199 --> 00:12:06.381
runs to more than eighty-five thousand volumes.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.560
Verne gives Fogg a house here — number seven.

00:12:20.915 --> 00:12:23.582
Here is what he does not tell you about the street.

00:12:24.981 --> 00:12:28.949
Two hundred and eighty metres, one block behind Regent Street.

00:12:30.895 --> 00:12:32.666
You can walk it in three minutes.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:36.809
The tailors arrived around eighteen-oh-three,

00:12:36.809 --> 00:12:40.274
following the army officers who already lived here

00:12:40.274 --> 00:12:45.957
— which is why the Savile Row shape comes from military uniform, not from fashion.

00:12:50.437 --> 00:12:53.980
The street will tell you the word bespoke was born here:

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.941
cloth spoken for by a customer.

00:12:58.532 --> 00:12:59.577
It was not.

00:13:00.295 --> 00:13:05.586
The word is from seventeen fifty-five, fifty years before the first tailor arrived.

00:13:08.088 --> 00:13:11.267
And in two thousand and eight they lost it for real.

00:13:12.881 --> 00:13:18.538
The advertising regulator ruled that a machine-sewn suit made in Germany could still

00:13:18.538 --> 00:13:19.750
be called bespoke.

00:13:22.914 --> 00:13:24.514
Nobody owns the term.

00:13:25.466 --> 00:13:30.287
So the Row stopped defending the dictionary and started defending geography.

00:13:32.591 --> 00:13:36.798
Its own standard now demands a paper pattern cut for one person,

00:13:36.798 --> 00:13:39.428
fifty hours of hand work, three fittings

00:13:39.428 --> 00:13:43.044
— and a workshop within a hundred yards of this street.

00:13:47.524 --> 00:13:49.828
One last thing about Fogg living here.

00:13:51.075 --> 00:13:53.864
He leaves for three months with a single bag:

00:13:53.864 --> 00:13:56.344
two shirts and three pairs of stockings.

00:13:58.836 --> 00:14:02.844
He lived on the street where one suit takes fifty hours by hand

00:14:02.844 --> 00:14:05.897
— and he packed as though clothes did not exist.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:23.581
At the top of Fogg's street is the room where Britain worked out which parts of the

00:14:23.581 --> 00:14:25.016
world were still unmapped.

00:14:27.603 --> 00:14:33.609
From eighteen seventy the Royal Geographical Society had its headquarters at number

00:14:33.609 --> 00:14:36.866
one Savile Row — where expeditions to Africa,

00:14:36.866 --> 00:14:40.702
Asia and the South Pole were planned and argued over.

00:14:45.182 --> 00:14:50.843
In April eighteen seventy-four, David Livingstone's body lay in that building before

00:14:50.843 --> 00:14:52.798
it went to Westminster Abbey.

00:14:55.998 --> 00:15:00.696
Six doors down the same street, Verne had just put a man who bet he could go round

00:15:00.696 --> 00:15:02.931
the world without looking at any of it.

00:15:05.869 --> 00:15:10.264
One end of a three-minute street: the people who wanted to see everything.

00:15:12.229 --> 00:15:16.432
The other end: a man who crosses the planet and notices nothing.

00:15:18.324 --> 00:15:22.309
The Society sold the building to a tailor in nineteen twelve

00:15:22.309 --> 00:15:26.559
— partly because that firm had dressed so many of the explorers.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:43.992
He begins a journey round the world from the exact spot Britain measures every

00:15:43.992 --> 00:15:44.888
distance from.

00:15:47.663 --> 00:15:48.879
The third address.

00:15:49.674 --> 00:15:51.914
A bronze horseman at Charing Cross.

00:15:53.143 --> 00:15:57.921
Since the early eighteen hundreds it has been the point every road distance to

00:15:57.921 --> 00:15:59.330
London is counted from.

00:16:02.231 --> 00:16:06.711
When a sign in Scotland says London four hundred, it means this statue.

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:09.998
Not the city.

00:16:10.748 --> 00:16:11.921
This statue.

00:16:12.698 --> 00:16:16.709
There is a stone cross in the station forecourt that looks medieval.

00:16:18.688 --> 00:16:21.632
It is Victorian, and it is in the wrong place.

00:16:23.159 --> 00:16:25.719
The real one stood where the horseman is now.

00:16:27.084 --> 00:16:28.769
It was the last of twelve.

00:16:29.763 --> 00:16:32.800
When Eleanor of Castile died in twelve ninety,

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:36.960
her body was carried a hundred and eighty miles to Westminster,

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:41.582
and Edward the First built a cross at every place it rested overnight.

00:16:46.062 --> 00:16:50.862
Charing was the final night, and its cross cost seven times any of the others.

00:16:53.175 --> 00:16:58.574
It came down in sixteen forty-seven, after standing three hundred and fifty-three

00:16:58.574 --> 00:17:03.906
years, and the parish sold the stone — forty-seven cartloads of it — for paving.

00:17:08.386 --> 00:17:11.543
Fogg's train leaves from a station built on that spot.

00:17:13.161 --> 00:17:18.390
And at a quarter to nine, a man walks onto a platform and starts measuring something

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:19.262
rather larger.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.816
He gave himself ten minutes to pack for the planet.

00:17:36.192 --> 00:17:39.856
That same morning Fogg had hired a new manservant:

00:17:39.856 --> 00:17:43.960
a Frenchman called Jean Passepartout — a former acrobat,

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:49.530
rope-dancer and Paris fireman who had taken a valet's job because he wanted,

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.021
in his own words, a tranquil life.

00:17:56.501 --> 00:17:58.549
He had been in the house nine hours.

00:17:59.627 --> 00:18:02.876
Fogg leaves the club at twenty-five past seven,

00:18:02.876 --> 00:18:07.786
tells him they are going round the world and will start in ten minutes,

00:18:07.786 --> 00:18:13.110
and packs one carpet-bag: two shirts, three pairs of stockings, a mackintosh,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.392
a cloak, and a railway timetable.

00:18:19.872 --> 00:18:22.816
The train for Dover leaves at a quarter to nine.

00:18:24.242 --> 00:18:25.437
It is raining.

00:18:26.182 --> 00:18:29.433
His watch is four minutes slow and set to London,

00:18:29.433 --> 00:18:32.817
and he has no intention of changing it for anybody.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:39.604
And somewhere behind them, in an empty house on Savile Row,

00:18:39.604 --> 00:18:42.928
a gas lamp is still burning that nobody turned off

00:18:42.928 --> 00:18:47.051
— which is going to be funny for about seventy-nine more days.

00:18:51.531 --> 00:18:54.070
Ahead of him: a boat across the Channel.

00:18:55.339 --> 00:18:58.582
A hole under the Alps that opened thirteen months ago.

00:19:00.124 --> 00:19:01.895
A canal three years old.

00:19:02.865 --> 00:19:05.617
A railway across India with a piece missing.

00:19:06.968 --> 00:19:09.208
An ocean that takes twenty-two days.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.628
And somewhere out on the Pacific, something quietly happens to the calendar that

00:19:15.628 --> 00:19:17.208
nobody on board notices.

00:19:20.154 --> 00:19:21.285
Eighty days.

00:19:22.006 --> 00:19:23.350
He has already gone.
